  "answerSpec": {
   "value": 0.1111111111111111,
   "tolerance": {
    "kind": "absolute",
    "amount": 0.0005
   }
  },
  "explanation": "Let $u = \\sqrt{x}$: $3u^{2} + 5u - 2 = 0$, so $(3u - 1)(u + 2) = 0$. $u = -2$ is rejected because $\\sqrt{x} \\geq 0$. So $\\sqrt{x} = \\frac{1}{3}$ and $x = \\frac{1}{9}$.",
  "workedSolution": "1. Let $u = \\sqrt{x}$, so $x = u^{2}$: $3u^{2} + 5u - 2 = 0$.\n2. $(3u - 1)(u + 2) = 0$, so $u = \\frac{1}{3}$ or $u = -2$.\n3. $\\sqrt{x} = -2$ is impossible (the square root is never negative): reject.\n4. $\\sqrt{x} = \\frac{1}{3}$ gives $x = \\frac{1}{9}$. Check: $\\frac{1}{3} + \\frac{5}{3} - 2 = 0$.",
  "hints": [
   "Let u = √x.",
   "Which value of u is impossible?"
  ],
  "modelResponse": {
